{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "text": "What is a default value of a parameter?",
      "options": [
        "The value the parameter gets when the call passes nothing for it",
        "The value the parameter keeps for good, whatever the call passes",
        "The first value the function prints",
        "The value the function returns when it has no return"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "def greet(name, word=\"Hello\"):\n    print(f\"{word}, {name}!\")\n\ngreet(\"Tommy\")",
      "options": [
        "Hello, Tommy!",
        "word, Tommy!",
        "It stops with an error: word is missing",
        "Hello, name!"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "def greet(name, word=\"Hello\"):\n    print(f\"{word}, {name}!\")\n\ngreet(\"Tommy\", \"Hi\")",
      "options": [
        "Hello, Tommy!",
        "Hi, Tommy!",
        "Hello Hi, Tommy!",
        "It stops with an error: word already has a value"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "In `def create_player(name, health=100, city=\"Vice City\"):`, which argument does every call have to pass?",
      "options": ["health", "city", "name", "None: all three have a default"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "def f(a=10, b, c):\n    print(a, b, c)\n\nf(2, 3)",
      "options": [
        "It prints 10 2 3",
        "It prints 2 3 10",
        "It prints 10 None None",
        "It stops with a SyntaxError: b and c have no default but come after a, which has one"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "text": "Which of these `def` lines is allowed?",
      "options": [
        "def f(a=1, b):",
        "def f(a=1, b, c=3):",
        "def f(a, b=2, c=3):",
        "def f(a, b=2, c):"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "def create_player(name, health=100, city=\"Vice City\"):\n    print(f\"{name} | HP: {health} | City: {city}\")\n\ncreate_player(\"Tommy\", city=\"Liberty City\")",
      "options": [
        "Tommy | HP: 100 | City: Liberty City",
        "Tommy | HP: Liberty City | City: Vice City",
        "Tommy | HP: 100 | City: Vice City",
        "It stops with an error: health was skipped"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "Why does `create_player(\"Tommy\", city=\"Liberty City\")` name `city`?",
      "options": [
        "Because a text can only go into a parameter by its name",
        "To change the default of city for every later call",
        "Because city is the last parameter, and the last one always needs its name",
        "To skip health, which keeps its default, and still set city"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q9",
      "text": "Without a keyword argument, which call prints the same as `create_player(\"Tommy\", city=\"Liberty City\")`?",
      "options": [
        "create_player(\"Tommy\", \"Liberty City\")",
        "create_player(\"Tommy\", \"Liberty City\", 100)",
        "create_player(\"Tommy\", 100, \"Liberty City\")",
        "create_player(\"Liberty City\", \"Tommy\")"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q10",
